PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2}

I agree with colleagues 1 and 3 that B2 is the unique forcing first move. The two black clusters (C1-C2 with liberties at B1/B2 and A2-B3-B4 with liberties at A1/A3/A4/B2) are linked only through the shared B2 point. Playing B2 immediately puts the lower group in atari; Black’s only reply is B1, which joins the groups but leaves the combined shape with just three liberties (A1, A3, A4). White can then answer at A3 or A4, reducing to two liberties and continuing the capture sequence regardless of Black’s replies.  

C5, while it does pressure the upper stones, leaves Black an immediate saving extension at A3 or B2 that restores connectivity and adds liberties, so it does not start a forced kill. Likewise, any first move at B1 allows Black to answer at B2, connecting everything and keeping at least four liberties, after which White cannot force capture. The liberty-counting and connection logic therefore singles out B2 as the only move that initiates a kill no matter how Black responds.

1.text:
{B2}

B2 is the unique forcing first move. It ataris the C1-C2 pair (only liberty left: B1). Black’s only reply is B1, which connects the two black groups but leaves the combined shape with just three liberties (A1/A3/A4). White then fills those liberties in succession and captures regardless of Black’s order of replies.  

Any other candidate (C5, A3, A4, B1, etc.) either leaves an extra liberty or permits Black a saving reply that keeps the group alive, so they do not initiate a forced capture sequence. This matches the liberty-counting analysis already given by colleagues 1 and 3; colleague 2’s C5 suggestion does not produce a forced kill.

